// The Discord library: a bot-token capability plus the two planes a chat surface has — MESSAGES
// (in and out) and INTERACTIONS (one blocking question) — the Discord twin of the Slack package. The
// externals are implemented in `discord.ts` (the same module path, so they register under `discord.*`).
// The one-line integration is the provider:
// `use discord.provider(source = credentials.env(key = "DISCORD_TOKEN"))`.
//
// EVERY EXTERNAL TAKES THE TOKEN, and nothing here carries a handle to a live connection. That is the
// rule 0.7.0 was rebuilt on: a durable program may hold only durable values, and a pointer into the
// sidecar's process memory is not one. A token plus a channel is a SELF-SUFFICIENT reference — any
// process holding those two reaches the same channel — so a runtime restart costs the interrupted call
// and nothing else: re-fork the watcher and it connects again. (0.6.0 served an opaque client handle
// from a `connection` request, and a restart handed the program back a handle whose client was gone,
// so every later call through it panicked and re-forking looped on the same dead handle.) The sidecar
// still shares one gateway socket per token internally, refcounted, which no Katari value names and
// nothing can observe.
//
// Files are first-class on both directions: an incoming message's attachments arrive as `file` values
// (the sidecar downloads each and uploads it over the blob side channel), and `send_message` posts
// `file` values back as Discord attachments.
//
// The interaction plane is ONE agent, `ask`: it posts a prompt with `control` values in front of it and
// blocks for the `answer`. Approving an action, taking free-form text, editing a draft and picking from
// a list are that same call with different DATA — not four agents with four contracts.
//
// This module is the Discord half of a TWIN CONTRACT with the `slack` package: the same data types with
// the same fields and the same agents with the same argument names, so a bot ports between them by
// swapping the import. THE COMPLETE LIST OF DIVERGENCES LIVES IN ONE PLACE — the slack package's README,
// under "Divergences" — and is checked mechanically by `slack/scripts/check-twin.mjs`, which reads both
// modules and fails on a shape either side grew without the other. A second copy of that list here is a
// second copy that would go stale, which is what the twin contract was already losing to.

@"The Discord bot-token capability — provided at the root by `use discord.provider(...)`, resolved per
call, so an env rotation or an oauth refresh lands without a restart. It is the WHOLE of what this
package's calls need beside a channel: the token names the bot to Discord from any process, so nothing
served here can be invalidated by a restart."
request credential() -> string of private

@"Provide the Discord bot token for the extent of the continuation: serve `credential` from @source@,
resolved at EVERY ask (the source is a NAME — where the token lives — never a value, so a rotation
lands on the next call rather than on the next restart).

NOTHING IS CONNECTED HERE and nothing is closed on the way out, which is what changed in 0.7.0. A call
that needs the gateway (`watch_messages`, `ask`) opens one for its own lifetime inside the sidecar, so
a bad token surfaces THERE — as `discord_error` from the call that wanted it — rather than at this
install site, and this provider's only failures are the credential's own (`env.missing_secret` /
`oauth.server_error`)."
agent provider[R, effect E](
  @"Where the bot token lives (env key or oauth name); resolved at each `credential` ask." source: credentials.source,
  @"Runs with `credential` served; its result is the provider's result." continuation: agent (value: null) -> R with {...E, credential},
) -> R with E | io | prelude.throw[env.missing_secret | oauth.server_error] {
  use handler {
    request credential() -> string of private { next credentials.resolve(source = source) }
  }
  continuation(value = null)
}

@"An authentication / authorization failure: the bot token is invalid or expired (HTTP 401), or the
bot lacks permission in the channel (HTTP 403); @message@ carries Discord's error. The bot cannot
recover on its own — the operator must fix the token or the bot's permissions. Let it surface so the
bot stops loudly rather than silently dropping every reply. (A rotated token does not need a restart:
every call resolves the credential afresh.)"
data auth_error(message: string)

@"A Discord API call failed for a non-auth reason: a rate limit (HTTP 429), a channel that is not a
sendable text channel, a payload the platform rejects (a control past one of its caps), a transient
network fault; @message@ carries Discord's error. Usually per-message or transient, so a bot catches it
to drop just that reply and keep serving."
data api_error(message: string)

// A Discord send failure: an auth/authorization failure or a plain API failure. Raised as a
// `prelude.throw` (never a panic — that would tear the whole run down), classified in the sidecar by
// the HTTP status. (Type synonyms take no docs.)
type discord_error = auth_error | api_error

// ── the message plane: what arrives, and what goes out ───────────────────────────────────────────

@"One incoming message, as data: what arrived, where, from whom, and with what attached.
`watch_messages` delivers ONE of these rather than a spread of positional arguments, so a @deliver_to@
names the fields it reads, has no argument order to get wrong, and keeps its signature when the
delivered shape grows a field."
data message(
  @"The channel the message arrived on — hand it straight to `send_message` to reply in place." channel: string,
  @"The sender's raw Discord user id (a snowflake); see `watch_messages` on tagging it before it leaves the program." author: string,
  @"The name Discord's own client shows for the sender: their per-server nickname, else their global display name, else their username (total — a DM has no nickname to read, an account without a global name still has a username). SELF-CHOSEN, NOT UNIQUE, and NOT AN IDENTITY: anyone in a server may set their nickname to whatever another member's reads, so a matching name proves nothing about who spoke. @author@ is the identity — authorize on it (or on a tag derived from it), NEVER on this. Use this to ADDRESS someone, not to trust them." display_name: string,
  @"The message text; empty for an attachments-only post." text: string,
  @"The attachments, already downloaded into `file` values." files: array[file],
)

@"Low-level: post to a channel with the bot @token@, with any files as attachments, returning the
posted message's id. A plain REST call — no gateway, nothing kept between calls — so it behaves
identically before and after a restart. Raises `auth_error` or `api_error` (`discord_error`) when the
Discord API call fails. Prefer the send_message agent."
external agent discord_send(
  @"The bot token to post with." token: string of private,
  @"The channel to post into." channel: string,
  @"The message text; empty means an attachments-only post." text: string,
  @"Files to attach to the post." files: array[file],
) -> string with prelude.throw[discord_error]

@"Low-level: listen on a channel with the bot @token@ and deliver each incoming message as one
`message` value to a callback. The sidecar opens the gateway socket for THIS CALL'S lifetime (shared
with any other live watch or ask on the same token, closed when the last of them ends) and attaches the
listener before the socket comes up, so nothing that arrives on a fresh connection is delivered to
nobody. Raises `discord_error` when the gateway will not open — an invalid token is `auth_error`.
Prefer the watch_messages agent."
external agent discord_watch[effect E](
  @"The bot token to listen with." token: string of private,
  @"The channel to listen on." channel: string,
  @"Called once per incoming message; its effects E flow to the caller's handlers." deliver_to: agent (value: message) -> null with E,
) -> never with E | prelude.throw[discord_error]

@"Post a message to a channel with the ambient bot token, returning the posted MESSAGE'S ID —
the composable seam a later edit / reaction / thread-reply agent will address a message by, so a
caller that needs to refer back keeps it (and one that does not simply drops it). @files@ attach to
the post. Raises `discord_error` (`auth_error` | `api_error`) when the send fails — catch `api_error`
to keep a bot serving past a transient failure, and let `auth_error` surface so a bad token stops the
bot loudly."
agent send_message(
  @"The channel to post into." channel: string,
  @"The message text; empty means an attachments-only post." text: string,
  @"Files to attach; defaults to none." files: array[file] ?= [],
) -> string {
  discord_send(token = credential(), channel = channel, text = text, files = files)
}

@"A `try_send` that REACHED the channel: the message is posted. It carries nothing — a caller that
wants the posted message's id wants `send_message` and its own error policy."
data delivered()

@"A `try_send` that posted NOTHING, with @reason@ saying which of the two silences it was: the
transient `api_error` this agent swallowed (a rate limit, a channel the bot cannot post in, a channel
id that names nothing), or a blank text with no files. It is a VALUE rather than a silence because a
caller cannot tell the two apart from a `null`, and a program that reports \"posted\" for a post that
never landed teaches whoever reads it — a person, or a model — something false."
data dropped(@"Why nothing was posted, in one clause." reason: string)

// What became of one resilient post: it landed, or it did not and here is why. (Type synonyms take no docs.)
type send_outcome = delivered | dropped

@"Post RESILIENTLY, fire-and-forget — the composition every long-lived bot writes around
`send_message`, packaged: a blank @text@ with no @files@ posts nothing (a quiet protocol's silence);
a transient `api_error` (a rate limit, an unsendable channel) drops just this post so the bot keeps
serving; `auth_error` re-raises so a bad token still stops the bot loudly.

IT ANSWERS WITH THE OUTCOME (`delivered` | `dropped(reason)`), which is the whole of what changed in
0.5.0 and the reason it is a breaking change. The old `-> null` made the two endings indistinguishable
from the outside, so every caller that reported on its own send reported SUCCESS for a post the channel
never received — most damagingly a model-facing tool answering \"(posted)\" to an agent that then went
on believing it had spoken. Dropping the post is still right (one bad send must not end a resident bot);
dropping the FACT of it was not. The posted message's id is still not carried — a caller that needs it
wants `send_message`."
agent try_send(
  @"The channel to post into." channel: string,
  @"The message text; blank with no files posts nothing." text: string,
  @"Files to attach; defaults to none." files: array[file] ?= [],
) -> send_outcome {
  if (string.is_blank(value = text) && array.length(target = files) == 0) {
    dropped(reason = "the text was blank and no files were attached, so there was nothing to post")
  } else {
    use handler {
      request prelude.throw(error: discord_error) -> never {
        match (error) {
          case api_error(message => message) -> { break dropped(reason = f"Discord refused the post (${message})") }
          // The residual is exactly `auth_error` — rethrown as bound, no reconstruction.
          case rest -> { prelude.throw(error = rest) }
        }
      }
    }
    let _message_id = send_message(channel = channel, text = text, files = files)
    delivered()
  }
}

@"Serve a channel forever, delivering each incoming message to @deliver_to@ as one `message` value —
shaped like `time.watch` / `google_calendar.watch` so it composes under `parallel [ … ]`. Bot posts
(this bot's own replies included) are not delivered, so replying from @deliver_to@ cannot loop.
@author@ on the delivered message is the sender's raw Discord user id (a snowflake — an ENUMERABLE
numeric id, not a name); a plain digest of an enumerable id is dictionary-reversible, so pass it
through `author_tag` before letting it leave the program, e.g. as an AI provider's abuse-attribution
tag. That agent is this package's answer to a pseudonym every bot was otherwise writing (and getting
wrong) for itself: keyed with `crypto.hmac_sha256` under a named secret, so the tag cannot be walked
back to an account.

@display_name@ beside it is the name Discord SHOWS for that sender, and it is not a second identity: a
display name is SELF-CHOSEN and NOT UNIQUE — anyone in the server can set their nickname to read exactly
like the operator's, and a public channel is precisely where a stranger will. So a program that decides
what it may do from the name it was told has no authorization at all. Branch on @author@ (or on a tag
derived from it) and treat @display_name@ as what it is: how to ADDRESS the speaker.

Delivery is exactly once WITHIN one live gateway connection, and NOT GUARANTEED either way across a
reconnect — which is where this differs from the Slack twin's at-least-once. The gateway has no
per-event acknowledgement, so a message can be MISSED: an invalidated session, an outage past the
resume window, or this run restarting each re-identify, and Discord backfills nothing onto a fresh
session. A message can also be REPEATED, though far more rarely: a resume replays from the last
sequence the shard RECORDED, which it advances when an event ARRIVES rather than after @deliver_to@
returns. No dedup memory is kept here. A bot that must miss nothing reconciles against the channel's
own history rather than trusting this stream.

A runtime restart INTERRUPTS this call and costs nothing else, which is what changed in 0.7.0: the
token is the whole of what the sidecar needs, so a re-forked watch opens a new socket and serves the
same channel again. (0.6.0 handed the re-fork the opaque client handle it was still carrying, whose
client had died with the process, so every attempt panicked identically — a silent crash loop where a
plain `region.crashed` → fork again now works.) What the interruption costs is the messages that arrive
while nothing is watching, which is the reconnect gap above and not a new one.

It RAISES `discord_error` (`auth_error` | `api_error`) when the gateway will not open, because opening
it is now this call's job: `use discord.provider(...)` connects to nothing and so throws no
`discord_error` at all. An invalid token stops the watch as `auth_error` on its first attempt."
agent watch_messages[effect E](
  @"The channel to serve." channel: string,
  @"Called once per incoming message, with the whole message as one value; its effects E flow to the caller's handlers." deliver_to: agent (value: message) -> null with E,
) -> never with E | credential | io | prelude.throw[discord_error] {
  discord_watch(token = credential(), channel = channel, deliver_to = deliver_to)
}

// ── the interaction plane: the controls a question offers, and the answer one of them returns ────

@"A clickable button: pressing it IS the answer (`clicked`), with no second step. @id@ returns
verbatim, so a program branches on the id IT wrote rather than on the label a human reads — rename the
label freely and every branch still holds. Discord caps a label at 80 characters, and this package
SHORTENS a longer one (with a trailing `…`) rather than failing the question — a caption is
presentation, so it never decides whether an ask is askable."
data button(
  @"The answer's identity; returned verbatim as `clicked.id`. At most 100 characters, and NOT shortened — it is how Discord routes the press back, so an over-long or blank one fails the ask as `api_error` instead of being silently altered into a key that could collide with a sibling's." id: string,
  @"What the button reads on screen; at most 80 characters, shortened with `…` beyond that, and rendered as @id@ when blank." label: string,
)

@"A dropdown: picking one option IS the answer (`chose`), carrying the option's own text. Reach for it
when the options are DATA the program computed (the open tickets, the matching files); a FIXED set of
choices is clearer as one `button` each, because then every branch has an id the program named.
Discord caps a dropdown at 25 options of 100 characters, and renders @label@ as the placeholder line
shown while nothing is picked."
data select(
  @"The answer's identity; returned verbatim as `chose.id`. At most 100 characters, and NOT shortened — see `button`'s @id@." id: string,
  @"The placeholder line shown while nothing is picked; at most 150 characters, shortened with `…` beyond that. Blank leaves Discord's own default line in place." label: string,
  @"The options offered; the picked one returns as `chose.option`. Each is 1 to 100 characters and is NOT shortened: the option's own text IS the answer, so a clamped one would report a choice the program never offered — an over-long or blank option fails the ask as `api_error`." options: array[string],
)

@"One text box of a `form`: labelled, optionally prefilled, optionally multi-line. A PREFILLED box is
how a draft gets edited — the human reads the program's proposal and submits it changed or unchanged,
so the program never has to guess which parts were accepted. Discord caps a box's LABEL at 45
characters (shortened here rather than fatal) and its @value@ at 4000 (not shortened — see below)."
data field(
  @"The key this box's text lands under in `submitted.values`. At most 100 characters, and NOT shortened — it is the key the submitted text comes back under, so an over-long or blank one fails the ask as `api_error`." id: string,
  @"What the box is labelled on screen; at most 45 characters, shortened with `…` beyond that, and rendered as @id@ when blank." label: string,
  @"The text the box starts with — a draft to edit; defaults to empty. At most 4000 characters and NOT shortened: a submit means \"approved AT THESE VALUES\", so a draft quietly docked to fit would be approved as though it were whole — an over-long one fails the ask as `api_error`, for the program to shorten deliberately (or to post as a file and ask about instead)." value: string ?= "",
  @"Whether the box is a multi-line paragraph rather than one line; defaults to one line." multiline: boolean ?= false,
)

@"A form: a button that opens a dialog of @fields@, and SUBMITTING the dialog is the answer
(`submitted`), carrying every field's text. Two steps because that is Discord's physics — text input
exists only inside a dialog, and a dialog only opens in response to a click — so a form renders as one
button reading @label@ above a dialog titled @title@. Opening the dialog and closing it again is NOT
an answer: nothing was submitted, the question stays open, and any control (this one again, or a
sibling) can still answer it. Discord takes 1 to 5 fields."
data form(
  @"The answer's identity; returned verbatim as `submitted.id`. At most 94 characters — six shorter than any other id, because the dialog's own identifier carries a prefix — and NOT shortened; an over-long or blank one fails the ask as `api_error` where the question POSTS, before a human is asked to press anything." id: string,
  @"What the opening button reads on screen; at most 80 characters, shortened with `…` beyond that, and rendered as @id@ when blank." label: string,
  @"The dialog's title; keep it to 24 characters. That is the TWIN CONTRACT's bound, not Discord's own (Discord allows 45): a form written for one chat platform should render on the other, and Slack's dialog title caps at 24 — so the shared surface takes the lower of the two. Past 45 it is shortened with `…` here (Slack's twin rejects past 24 instead), and a blank title renders as @id@." title: string,
  @"The boxes the dialog presents, in order." fields: array[field],
)

// What a question may put in front of a human: an immediate button, a dropdown, or a form dialog.
// (Type synonyms take no docs.)
type control = button | select | form

@"A `button` was pressed."
data clicked(
  @"The pressed button's @id@." id: string,
  @"Who answered: their raw Discord user id (a snowflake)." by: string,
  @"What the answerer is CALLED, resolved as `message`'s @display_name@ is — nickname, else global display name, else username. SELF-CHOSEN, NOT UNIQUE, and NOT AN IDENTITY: another member can wear the same name at will. @by@ is who answered; authorize on that, never on this." display_name: string,
)

@"A `select`'s option was picked."
data chose(
  @"The dropdown's @id@." id: string,
  @"The picked option, verbatim from the `select`'s @options@." option: string,
  @"Who answered: their raw Discord user id (a snowflake)." by: string,
  @"What the answerer is CALLED, resolved as `message`'s @display_name@ is — nickname, else global display name, else username. SELF-CHOSEN, NOT UNIQUE, and NOT AN IDENTITY: another member can wear the same name at will. @by@ is who answered; authorize on that, never on this." display_name: string,
)

@"A `form` was submitted: @values@ carries every declared field's text, keyed by the field's @id@ (a
box left blank is present as the empty string, so the record is total over the form's fields). What
came back IS the decision, not a suggestion to re-derive: a submit means \"approved AT THESE VALUES\",
so acting on @values@ acts on exactly what the human read and sent — the click and the deed cannot
drift apart."
data submitted(
  @"The submitted form's @id@." id: string,
  @"Each field's text, keyed by field id." values: record[string],
  @"Who answered: their raw Discord user id (a snowflake)." by: string,
  @"What the answerer is CALLED, resolved as `message`'s @display_name@ is — nickname, else global display name, else username. SELF-CHOSEN, NOT UNIQUE, and NOT AN IDENTITY: another member can wear the same name at will. @by@ is who answered; authorize on that, never on this." display_name: string,
)

// Which control completed the question, and with what. (Type synonyms take no docs.)
type answer = clicked | chose | submitted

@"Low-level: post a prompt with its controls to a channel with the bot @token@ and await the first
completed interaction. The sidecar brings the gateway socket up BEFORE the question posts (Discord
delivers an interaction only to a session connected at the moment of the click) and lets it go when the
question ends. Prefer the ask agent."
external agent discord_ask(
  @"The bot token to ask with." token: string of private,
  @"The channel to ask in." channel: string,
  @"The question posted above the controls." prompt: string,
  @"The controls to present, in order." controls: array[control],
) -> answer with prelude.throw[discord_error]

@"Post @prompt@ with @controls@ as ONE message and BLOCK until someone in the channel COMPLETES one of
them, returning which control answered and with what — the human-in-the-loop step (approving an
irreversible action, editing a draft before it goes out, choosing among candidates, taking a note).
Those are not four agents: they are this call with different @controls@ data — two `button`s is an
approval, a `form` of one empty `field` is free-form text, a `form` of PREFILLED fields is draft
editing, a `select` is a pick from a computed list.

Ask in a channel whose MEMBERSHIP is the trust boundary: any member may answer, and the FIRST
COMPLETED interaction is the answer. Merely opening a form's dialog completes nothing, so a curious
click cannot consume the question. Once answered, the controls are stripped off the message and the
outcome left in their place, so the channel keeps a readable record and a late click has nothing to
press. A question that ends WITHOUT an answer is stripped the same way and left reading `(expired)`:
an expiry, a cancel or a teardown never abandons live controls in the channel for someone to press
into nothing. Stripping is best effort either way — if the edit itself fails the stale controls stay,
and clicking one gets Discord's own \"interaction failed\" notice.

What that record READS is the control's own text — a `button`'s or `form`'s @label@, a `select`'s
picked @option@ — so those strings are not merely what a human clicks: they become the channel's
lasting, public account of what was decided. Write them as the audit line you would want to find
later. The record names the answerer as a Discord MENTION (the channel renders it as a name), while
the @by@ the `answer` carries is the raw id — the readable form is for the channel, the correlatable
one for the program.

`ask` holds NO time limit, deliberately: a deadline is `time.with_deadline` wrapped around it and a
withdrawal is `region.cancel_by_id` on the fiber holding it. One blocking question composes with
both, whereas an expiry baked in here would make every caller handle a timeout it never asked for.

The wait rides one external call, so a runtime restart while it is open interrupts it under the
at-most-once rule (a catchable panic) and the controls already in the channel go stale — nothing is left
to strip them, so a late click gets Discord's own \"interaction failed\" notice. That is the DESIGNED
outcome, not a defect to absorb here: let the panic ride to your supervisor and ASK AGAIN — whoever
wanted the answer wants it still — rather than inventing an answer for an interaction that never landed.
Asking again is the whole recovery as of 0.7.0: the new ask resolves the token and opens its own socket,
with no connection left over from before to be dead.

Raises `discord_error` when a Discord call fails: opening the gateway (an invalid token is `auth_error`),
posting the question, or opening a form's dialog.

A control past one of Discord's caps splits two ways, and the split is what stops a cosmetic string
from being able to break a run. PRESENTATION is CLAMPED, never fatal: a `button`'s or `form`'s @label@
past 80 characters, a `select`'s @label@ past 150, a `form`'s @title@ past 45 and a `field`'s @label@
past 45 are shortened with a trailing `…`, and a BLANK one renders as the control's own @id@ (blank is
just as illegal to Discord as over-long, so it needs a substitute either way). What CARRIES MEANING is
CHECKED instead and fails the ask as `api_error`: an @id@ (at most 100 characters, 94 for a `form` —
it is the routing key, and two shortened ids could collide and answer the wrong control), a `select`
option (1 to 100 — the option's text IS the answer), and a `field`'s @value@ (at most 4000 — the draft
a human submits as approved). Whatever this package cannot check locally is still the platform
rejecting the payload and still `api_error`: a sixth row, a 26th option, a dialog past five fields.

RUN `check_controls` WHERE THE CONTROLS ARE BUILT, not here. It answers every one of those caps —
including the counts above and the duplicate @id@ neither side catches — as a VALUE, purely, with no
credential and no connection needed: a gate's constructor can refuse itself before a run ever starts.
It is not wired into this agent on purpose. `ask` returns an `answer` and has no room in that type for a verdict, so
an internal check could only THROW — and a cosmetic string that can throw is the exact failure this
package's clamping was written to remove. So the verdict stays a value, `ask` stays unchanged, and
what a failed check means is the caller's decision.

@by@ on the answer is the answerer's raw Discord user id (a snowflake — an ENUMERABLE numeric id, not
a name); a plain digest of an enumerable id is dictionary-reversible, so pass it through `author_tag`
before letting it leave the program. The @display_name@ beside
it is what that answerer is CALLED, and a name is SELF-CHOSEN and NOT UNIQUE — a channel's other
members can wear it too. A question whose answer depends on WHO gave it must read @by@; a name that
decided anything would let whoever renamed themselves decide it instead."
agent ask(
  @"The channel to ask in — its membership is who may answer." channel: string,
  @"The question posted above the controls." prompt: string,
  @"What the human may complete: `button` / `select` / `form` values, rendered in order. Each control's @id@ must be DISTINCT within one ask — the id is the correlation key Discord carries back, so a repeat is unroutable rather than merely untidy (the sidecar keeps the first and warns). Discord fits at most 5 rows on a message: up to 5 buttons share a row, each dropdown takes one of its own. `check_controls` answers all of this as a value, before the question posts." controls: array[control],
) -> answer with credential | io | prelude.throw[discord_error] {
  discord_ask(token = credential(), channel = channel, prompt = prompt, controls = controls)
}

// ── the platform's numbers, and a local check against them ───────────────────────────────────────
//
// Every cap Discord enforces used to live in ONE place: the sidecar's renderer. So a program could
// not ASK what a cap was, and learned what it had broken only where the payload was refused — at the
// moment a human pressed a button, hours after the gate that built the control was written. `limits`
// publishes the numbers as data and `check_controls` reads them, so "is this question askable?" is a
// VALUE a program computes before the question posts, next to the code that built the controls.
//
// Both are PURE: no credential, no io, nothing to catch. A check that needed the gateway would be a
// check nobody runs at construction time, which is the only time it is worth running.

@"Discord's limits on what ONE question may present, as data — the numbers this package's renderer
clamps and checks against, published so a program can read them instead of rediscovering them from a
refused payload. Lengths are in CHARACTERS; the renderer counts UTF-16 code units and Katari's
`string.length` counts code points, so the two disagree by one per astral character (an emoji outside
the BMP) — a control that sits within a couple of characters of its cap should be shortened rather
than trusted to either count.

Each field says what happens PAST it, because the split is the whole design (see `ask`): a
presentation string is CLAMPED and a load-bearing one is FATAL. `check_controls` reports both, since
a clamp is silent and a program that wanted its own words posted still wants to know."
data caps(
  @"One MESSAGE's text: at most this many characters, FATAL past it. It is the cap on an `ask`'s @prompt@ (a question is one message) and on `send_message` / `try_send`'s @text@ — and it is the one number here that no control carries, which is why `check_controls` cannot see it: a caller building a prompt out of text it did not write must bound that text ITSELF, against this. Past it Discord refuses the whole post, so an over-long prompt is a question that can never be asked and therefore a gate that can never be granted." message_text: integer,
  @"A `button`'s @label@ — and a `form`'s opening button's: at most this many characters, CLAMPED past it." button_label: integer,
  @"A `select`'s @label@, the placeholder line: at most this many characters, CLAMPED past it. Blank is legal here alone — it leaves Discord's own default line." select_label: integer,
  @"Each of a `select`'s @options@: 1 to this many characters, FATAL outside it — the option's text IS the answer." select_option: integer,
  @"How many @options@ one `select` may offer: 1 to this many, FATAL outside it. The platform's own number, which no local builder checks." select_options: integer,
  @"A `form`'s @title@: at most this many characters, CLAMPED past it. Discord's own number — the twin contract asks for 24, which is a convention rather than a cap (see `form`'s @title@)." form_title: integer,
  @"How many `field` boxes one `form`'s dialog may hold: 1 to this many, FATAL outside it. The platform's own number, which no local builder checks — a sixth box passes every build and comes back a 400 when the operator clicks." form_fields: integer,
  @"A `field`'s @label@: at most this many characters, CLAMPED past it." field_label: integer,
  @"A `field`'s @value@, the prefilled draft: at most this many characters, FATAL past it — a submit means \"approved AT THESE VALUES\", so a docked draft would be approved as though it were whole. Blank is legal: it is the absence of a prefill." field_value: integer,
  @"A `button`'s, `select`'s or `field`'s @id@: 1 to this many characters, FATAL outside it — it is the routing key, never presentation." control_id: integer,
  @"A `form`'s @id@: 1 to this many characters, FATAL outside it. Shorter than @control_id@ because the form's DIALOG carries a prefix on the same identifier." form_id: integer,
  @"How many rows of controls one message may carry, FATAL past it." rows: integer,
  @"How many buttons share one row — the layout rule that decides how many of @rows@ a control list occupies: buttons pack this many to a row, and each `select` takes a row of its own." buttons_per_row: integer,
)

@"Discord's caps, as one `caps` value — pure, so it costs nothing and composes anywhere (a gate
builder, a test, an error message that wants to say what the bound was).

These numbers are DUPLICATED, deliberately and visibly: the renderer that enforces them lives in the
TypeScript sidecar, where the builders throw, and nothing in Katari can read a constant out of it.
The duplicate is kept honest by `scripts/check-limits.mjs`, which fails when this agent and the
sidecar's `LIMIT` disagree, so the two cannot drift apart in silence — the alternative (an external
agent that asks the sidecar) would make reading a constant an io call, and a check that needs a live
connection is a check nobody runs while writing the control."
agent limits() -> caps {
  caps(
    message_text = 2000,
    button_label = 80,
    select_label = 150,
    select_option = 100,
    select_options = 25,
    form_title = 45,
    form_fields = 5,
    field_label = 45,
    field_value = 4000,
    control_id = 100,
    form_id = 94,
    rows = 5,
    buttons_per_row = 5,
  )
}

@"The controls are askable exactly as written: every string fits, every count fits, every id is
distinct, and nothing would be shortened or substituted on the way to the channel."
data valid()

@"Something about the controls will not reach the channel as written, and @reason@ names WHICH
control and WHAT in one line — the first problem found, in declaration order, so a fix at a time
converges."
data invalid(@"Which control, and what is wrong with it." reason: string)

// The verdict `check_controls` returns: askable as written, or the first reason it is not.
// (Type synonyms take no docs.)
type control_check = valid | invalid

@"Check @controls@ against Discord's caps LOCALLY and answer with a VALUE — `valid`, or `invalid`
carrying one sentence naming the offending control. Pure: no credential, no io, nothing to catch, so
it belongs where the controls are BUILT (a gate's constructor, a test) rather than where they are
asked.

It is deliberately NOT wired into `ask`, and that is the lesson of the outage this exists for. `ask`
returns an `answer`; there is no room in that type for a verdict, so an internal check could only
THROW — and a cosmetic string that can throw is exactly the failure mode this package spent a release
removing (a 67-character label once restarted a resident system on every attempt). So the check is a
separate agent whose result is data, `ask`'s behaviour is unchanged, and a caller decides what a
failed check means: refuse the gate, shorten the label, log and ask anyway.

What it reports is wider than what `ask` FAILS on, on purpose:

- FATAL, as `ask` would: a blank or over-long @id@, a blank or over-long `select` option, a dropdown
  with no options or past `limits`' @select_options@, a dialog with no boxes or past @form_fields@,
  an over-long `field` @value@, a control list that needs more than @rows@ rows, and an EMPTY
  @controls@ (a question nobody can answer, which Discord posts happily).
- SILENT, which `ask` survives and a program still wants to know: a label, placeholder or title past
  its cap is SHORTENED with an ellipsis, and a blank one renders as the control's own @id@ — so the
  channel's lasting record of what was decided reads as an internal key, or as most of a sentence.
- DUPLICATE ids, which nothing else catches at all. Two controls sharing an @id@ cannot be told apart
  on the way back: Discord returns the id and nothing more. The sidecar keeps the FIRST and warns, so
  pressing the second control completes the first control's answer — \"deny\" answering as \"approve\".
  A `form`'s @fields@ are checked the same way, since `submitted.values` is keyed by field id and two
  boxes sharing one would collapse into a single entry.

Lengths are counted in Unicode CODE POINTS while the renderer counts UTF-16 units, so an astral
character (an emoji outside the BMP) is one here and two there; a string within a character or two of
its cap should be shortened rather than argued about.

```katari
let controls = [discord.button(id = \"approve\", label = \"approve\"), discord.button(id = \"deny\", label = \"deny\")]
match (discord.check_controls(controls = controls)) {
  case discord.valid() -> discord.ask(channel = channel, prompt = prompt, controls = controls)
  case discord.invalid(reason => reason) -> refuse_to_open_the_gate(why = reason)
}
```"
agent check_controls(
  @"The controls one `ask` would present, in the order it would render them." controls: array[control],
) -> control_check {
  let cap = limits()

  // A PRESENTATION string, which the renderer repairs rather than refuses: blank is substituted with
  // the control's own id and over-long is shortened with an ellipsis. Neither loses the run, and
  // both are SILENT — so the report says what would be posted instead of what was written.
  agent clamped_label(where: string, what: string, value: string, allowed: integer) -> string {
    if (value == "") {
      f"${where}: ${what} is blank, so the control would be labelled with its own id"
    } else if (string.length(value = value) > allowed) {
      f"${where}: ${what} is ${string.length(value = value)} characters where Discord takes ${allowed}, so it would be posted shortened with an ellipsis"
    } else {
      ""
    }
  }

  // The one presentation string whose BLANK is meaningful: a `select` with no placeholder leaves
  // Discord's own default line, which reads better than a substituted identifier.
  agent optional_label(where: string, what: string, value: string, allowed: integer) -> string {
    if (string.length(value = value) > allowed) {
      f"${where}: ${what} is ${string.length(value = value)} characters where Discord takes ${allowed}, so it would be posted shortened with an ellipsis"
    } else {
      ""
    }
  }

  // A LOAD-BEARING string: an id, an option. Shortening it would change what the question MEANS, so
  // the renderer refuses instead — blank as hard as over-long, and the ask fails as `api_error`.
  agent fatal_string(where: string, what: string, value: string, allowed: integer) -> string {
    if (value == "") {
      f"${where}: ${what} is blank, which fails the ask as api_error"
    } else if (string.length(value = value) > allowed) {
      f"${where}: ${what} is ${string.length(value = value)} characters where Discord takes ${allowed}, which fails the ask as api_error"
    } else {
      ""
    }
  }

  // Load-bearing, but legitimately absent: a `field`'s prefill. Empty means \"no draft\"; over-long
  // is fatal, because a draft docked to fit would be approved as though it were whole.
  agent optional_text(where: string, what: string, value: string, allowed: integer) -> string {
    if (string.length(value = value) > allowed) {
      f"${where}: ${what} is ${string.length(value = value)} characters where Discord takes ${allowed}, which fails the ask as api_error"
    } else {
      ""
    }
  }

  // The first non-empty reason, or "" — "" is how every check above spells \"nothing wrong\", and no
  // real reason is empty. First in declaration order wins, so a caller fixes one thing at a time.
  agent first_problem(problems: array[string]) -> string {
    for (let problem in problems, var found: string = "") {
      if (found == "" && problem != "") {
        next with { found = problem }
      } else {
        next
      }
    } then (_problems) {
      found
    }
  }

  // A control's own id, whichever constructor it is — the correlation key, and how a report names it.
  agent id_of(control: control) -> string {
    match (control) {
      case button(id => id) -> id
      case select(id => id) -> id
      case form(id => id) -> id
    }
  }

  // Whether the control takes a row of its own: Discord allows nothing beside a dropdown.
  agent takes_own_row(control: control) -> boolean {
    match (control) {
      case select(id => _) -> true
      case rest -> false
    }
  }

  // Which control a reason is about. The id names it where there is one; a blank id has only its
  // position, and the position is stated either way because two controls may share an id — which is
  // itself one of the things reported.
  agent where_of(index: integer, id: string) -> string {
    if (id == "") {
      f"control #${index + 1}"
    } else {
      f"control #${index + 1} (\"${id}\")"
    }
  }

  // How many rows these controls occupy, laid out exactly as the renderer lays them out: buttons pack
  // `buttons_per_row` to a row, a `form` contributes the BUTTON that opens its dialog and so packs
  // like any other, and a `select` flushes the pending buttons and takes a row alone.
  agent rows_used(controls: array[control]) -> integer {
    for (let control in controls, var rows: integer = 0, var pending: integer = 0) {
      if (takes_own_row(control = control)) {
        if (pending > 0) {
          next with { rows = rows + 2, pending = 0 }
        } else {
          next with { rows = rows + 1 }
        }
      } else if (pending == cap.buttons_per_row) {
        next with { rows = rows + 1, pending = 1 }
      } else {
        next with { pending = pending + 1 }
      }
    } then (_controls) {
      if (pending > 0) {
        rows + 1
      } else {
        rows
      }
    }
  }

  // A dropdown's options: the COUNT the platform enforces and no local builder checks, then each
  // option's own text, which is the answer itself and therefore never shortened.
  agent options_problem(where: string, options: array[string]) -> string {
    let count = array.length(target = options)
    if (count == 0) {
      f"${where}: the dropdown offers no options; Discord takes 1 to ${cap.select_options}"
    } else if (count > cap.select_options) {
      f"${where}: the dropdown offers ${count} options; Discord takes 1 to ${cap.select_options}"
    } else {
      for (let option in options, var at: integer = 0, var found: string = "") {
        let problem = fatal_string(where = where, what = f"option #${at + 1}", value = option, allowed = cap.select_option)
        if (found == "" && problem != "") {
          next with { at = at + 1, found = problem }
        } else {
          next with { at = at + 1 }
        }
      } then (_options) {
        found
      }
    }
  }

  // A dialog's boxes: the COUNT (again the platform's, checked by nothing local), each box's strings,
  // and the box ids' DISTINCTNESS — `submitted.values` is keyed by field id, so two boxes sharing one
  // come back as a single entry and the other box's text is simply gone.
  agent fields_problem(where: string, fields: array[field]) -> string {
    let count = array.length(target = fields)
    if (count == 0) {
      f"${where}: the dialog has no boxes; Discord takes 1 to ${cap.form_fields}"
    } else if (count > cap.form_fields) {
      f"${where}: the dialog has ${count} boxes; Discord takes 1 to ${cap.form_fields}"
    } else {
      for (let box in fields, var at: integer = 0, var seen: record[boolean] = record.empty(), var found: string = "") {
        let what = f"box #${at + 1}"
        let duplicate = if (box.id != "" && record.has(target = seen, key = box.id)) {
          f"${where}: ${what} repeats the field id \"${box.id}\", so both boxes would come back as one entry of submitted.values"
        } else {
          ""
        }
        let problem = first_problem(problems = [
          fatal_string(where = where, what = f"${what}'s id", value = box.id, allowed = cap.control_id),
          duplicate,
          clamped_label(where = where, what = f"${what}'s label", value = box.label, allowed = cap.field_label),
          optional_text(where = where, what = f"${what}'s prefill", value = box.value, allowed = cap.field_value),
        ])
        if (found == "" && problem != "") {
          next with { at = at + 1, seen = record.set(target = seen, key = box.id, value = true), found = problem }
        } else {
          next with { at = at + 1, seen = record.set(target = seen, key = box.id, value = true) }
        }
      } then (_fields) {
        found
      }
    }
  }

  // One control's own strings, in the order a reader would want them: the id first (it decides
  // whether the control can be routed at all), then what a human reads.
  agent control_problem(where: string, control: control) -> string {
    match (control) {
      case button(id => id, label => label) -> first_problem(problems = [
        fatal_string(where = where, what = "the id", value = id, allowed = cap.control_id),
        clamped_label(where = where, what = "the button's label", value = label, allowed = cap.button_label),
      ])
      case select(id => id, label => label, options => options) -> first_problem(problems = [
        fatal_string(where = where, what = "the id", value = id, allowed = cap.control_id),
        optional_label(where = where, what = "the dropdown's placeholder", value = label, allowed = cap.select_label),
        options_problem(where = where, options = options),
      ])
      case form(id => id, label => label, title => title, fields => fields) -> first_problem(problems = [
        fatal_string(where = where, what = "the id", value = id, allowed = cap.form_id),
        clamped_label(where = where, what = "the opening button's label", value = label, allowed = cap.button_label),
        clamped_label(where = where, what = "the dialog's title", value = title, allowed = cap.form_title),
        fields_problem(where = where, fields = fields),
      ])
    }
  }

  if (array.length(target = controls) == 0) {
    invalid(reason = "no controls: Discord posts the prompt happily and the question can never be answered")
  } else {
    for (let control in controls, var at: integer = 0, var seen: record[boolean] = record.empty(), var found: string = "") {
      let id = id_of(control = control)
      let where = where_of(index = at, id = id)
      // A blank id is reported by `control_problem` as the fatal thing it is, so it is not ALSO
      // reported as a duplicate of the previous blank one — the first report is the actionable one.
      let duplicate = if (id != "" && record.has(target = seen, key = id)) {
        f"${where}: two controls share this id, and Discord carries back nothing else — the first one keeps it, so completing this control would answer as the first"
      } else {
        ""
      }
      let problem = first_problem(problems = [duplicate, control_problem(where = where, control = control)])
      if (found == "" && problem != "") {
        next with { at = at + 1, seen = record.set(target = seen, key = id, value = true), found = problem }
      } else {
        next with { at = at + 1, seen = record.set(target = seen, key = id, value = true) }
      }
    } then (_controls) {
      if (found != "") {
        invalid(reason = found)
      } else if (rows_used(controls = controls) > cap.rows) {
        invalid(reason = f"the controls need ${rows_used(controls = controls)} rows and Discord fits ${cap.rows} on a message: buttons pack ${cap.buttons_per_row} to a row and each dropdown takes one of its own")
      } else {
        valid()
      }
    }
  }
}

@"Shorten @text@ so it fits in ONE Discord message, ending it with @marker@ when anything was cut —
the arithmetic every caller was doing against `limits()` by hand, and the half of that job which is
mechanical rather than editorial.

WHY IT IS NOT MERELY A SLICE. Past `limits()`'s @message_text@ Discord REFUSES the post rather than
shortening it, so an over-long prompt is a question that can never be asked (a gate that can never be
granted, failing identically on every retry) and an over-long reply is a message that never lands. The
room is therefore @message_text@ MINUS the marker's own length, because the marker is part of what gets
posted — measuring the text, cutting it to the cap and appending afterwards is the one way this is got
wrong, and it puts the result back over the cap every time.

WHY THE MARKER IS YOURS. Only the caller knows what the reader needs told, but SOMETHING must be told:
text that was silently docked reads exactly like text that ended, and a model reading it will answer
from a fragment as though it had the whole. Write the marker as the FACT — \"… (cut: the first 1,900 of
7,400 characters)\" — rather than as a bare ellipsis, so whoever reads it knows to ask for the rest.

Text that already fits comes back UNCHANGED, with no marker, so this is safe to wrap around everything.
A @marker@ that alone exceeds the cap leaves no room for any text and comes back cut to the cap itself:
the one guarantee here is that the result fits.

Lengths are Katari code points and Discord counts UTF-16 units, so text within a couple of characters of
the cap should be given a longer marker rather than trusted to either count."
agent fit_message(
  @"The text to fit; returned unchanged when it already does." text: string,
  @"What to end the result with when anything was cut — your own words, saying that it was cut and ideally by how much." marker: string,
) -> string {
  let cap = limits().message_text
  if (string.length(value = text) <= cap) {
    text
  } else {
    let room = cap - string.length(value = marker)
    if (room <= 0) {
      string.slice(value = marker, start = 0, end = cap)
    } else {
      f"${string.slice(value = text, start = 0, end = room)}${marker}"
    }
  }
}

// ── the speaker, pseudonymised ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

@"Tag a Discord user id into a short, stable PSEUDONYM — the ~25 lines every bot that lets a speaker
reach a model or a log was writing for itself, with the same one way to get it wrong.

WHY NOT A PLAIN DIGEST. A snowflake is an ENUMERABLE numeric id: it is a timestamp, a worker, a
process and a counter, so the space a real id lives in is small enough to walk. `crypto.sha256` of
one is therefore reversible by dictionary — anyone holding the digest hashes candidate snowflakes
until one matches, and the pseudonym is a user id again. `crypto.hmac_sha256` under a secret KEY has
no such attack: without the key there is nothing to hash candidates with. That is the whole
difference between a pseudonym and an obfuscation, and it is one function call wide, which is exactly
why it kept being got wrong.

WHAT COMES OUT is safe to SHOW: @length@ hex characters (8 by default — 32 bits, opaque and short
enough to sit inline in a prompt or a log line), stable for as long as the key is, and meaningless to
anyone without the key. Rotating the key rotates every pseudonym, which is the intended way to sever
a correlation. Keep @length@ generous if the tag must be UNIQUE rather than merely readable: 8 hex
characters collide at around 2^16 distinct speakers by the birthday bound, so a full 64 is what a
program correlating abuse reports across a large server should carry, and a short one is what it
shows. The result is deliberately PUBLIC despite the private key — an HMAC reveals nothing about its
key, which is the sound declassification `crypto.hmac_sha256` documents.

THE KEY is named, not passed: a `credentials.source` (`credentials.env(key = ...)` or
`credentials.oauth(name = ...)`), so the secret's VALUE never enters the app's dataflow. It resolves
at EVERY call, like every other credential use in Katari, so a rotated key lands without a restart —
a bot tagging every message pays one credential read per message for that. Any secret with a stable
lifetime will do; the bot's own token is the usual choice, since it already exists and dies with the
bot's identity.

WHAT IT IS NOT is an anonymisation. A pseudonym is stable by construction, so it links everything one
person ever said; what it removes is the ability to turn that link back into an account. Whether even
a pseudonymous link may cross to an AI provider is the app's judgement, exactly as with
`display_name` — this agent only makes the tag correct."
agent author_tag(
  @"Where the pseudonym key lives (an env key or an oauth credential name); resolved at each call, never held." source: credentials.source,
  @"The raw Discord user id to tag: a `message`'s @author@, or an `answer`'s @by@." author: string,
  @"How many hex characters to keep, from 1 to 64 (the full tag); defaults to 8 — short enough to read, wide enough to tell a channel's speakers apart. Out of range is clamped into it." length: integer ?= 8,
) -> string with io | prelude.throw[env.missing_secret | oauth.server_error] {
  let tagged = crypto.hmac_sha256(key = credentials.resolve(source = source), text = author)
  string.slice(value = tagged, start = 0, end = math.max(left = 1, right = math.min(left = length, right = 64)))
}
